# THE RULED SPELLING OF A LITERAL BRACE, MEASURED RATHER THAN CITED.
#
# `\{` and `\}` are the literal-brace spelling in the canonical text face. This
# file used to attribute that ruling to `docs/text-law.md` and to a census of
# "128 sites across 17 files" that document was said to enumerate. THAT
# DOCUMENT IS NOT IN THIS TREE (`gaps/GAP-109.md` records its loss), so neither
# the ruling nor the count could be read from where the file pointed. What
# survives the loss is `law.literal.text` — a hole holds literal AND expression
# segments — plus what the compiler still does, which is what the rows below
# are. The count is gone rather than restated: this file's numbers are its rows.
#
# THE RULING'S PAYOFF IS A DIAGNOSTIC, AND NO ROW HERE CAN HOLD IT. `d`'s
# literal spelled without protection warns
#
#   STR-1: `{b {x} c}` is not one whole expression, so it is emitted as
#          literal text
#   hint: ... if these braces are meant as text, write them `\{` and `\}`
#
# and answers `a{b {x} c}d` — nothing resolved. The file as committed is silent
# on both routes. The rows measure what that silence bought.
#
# `a` is the escape alone: `v=7` without it. `b` is one literal holding BOTH
# readings — the case no raw face can serve, and the control that ruling on
# `\{` did not buy literal braces by disabling holes. `c` is a brace pair that
# is not an expression at all.
#
# `d` and `e` had no program in this corpus and were carried only by unit tests
# in `src/parser.zig`. `d`: a protected brace must be invisible to the DEPTH
# counting in both directions, so `\{` may not deepen and `\}` may not close —
# unprotected, the `{x}` above is cut at the wrong `}` and stops resolving.
# `e`: protection is per DECODED BYTE, not per the two characters `\{`. `\x7B`
# is the byte `{` and opened a hole before the ruling — `A[\x7Bx}]` answered
# `A[7]`, and bare `A[{x}]` still does.
# expect: v={x} | both 7 and {x} | pair={"k": 1} | a{b 7 c}d | A[{x}]
# @corpus current

x = 7

a = "v=\{x}"
b = "both {x} and \{x}"
c = "pair=\{\"k\": 1\}"
d = "a\{b {x} c\}d"
e = "A[\x7Bx}]"

print("{a} | {b} | {c} | {d} | {e}")
0
